  "difficulty_reason": "The counted lower bound is not a routine application of the upper-bound constructions: the proof assigns to every prime power exactly dividing n a weight on each cycle of a generator, then shows that the total weight of each prime power is at least b(p^r), by a case analysis over the exceptional orders 2,3,4,5,7 and the exception families 6, 12 and 10-14, with the correction terms 11 and 20 when 3 and 2, respectively 3 and 4, exactly divide n. Reaching that point needs the source-local structural theory of cycles of a cyclic poset automorphism, including the extension remark and the four lemmas on how many cycles of length divisible by p^r, 4 or 2p a generator must contain, each proved by constructing an extra automorphism from a symmetry or complement of the height-one posets involved. The upper bound is equally constructive, combining the b(n)n-point posets with an ordinal sum and the exceptional 20-point poset for Z_12, so the exact minus-one correction is essential; this is well above ordinary PhD qualifying material.",
